        "pkcontributionId": 63132,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 172,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fallopian tube</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-25T15:29:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63133,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 172,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most women with these tumours are asymptomatic, and the majority of cases are an incidental finding at the time of surgery for another gynaecological disorder {3176920}. The adenofibromas are mainly located in the fimbria {9421082}. Papillomas may cause tubal obstruction or occur without symptoms {3721698} and are loosely attached to the tubal mucosa.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-25T15:29:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63134,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 172,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenofibromas are seen in 10% of women with <em>BRCA1/2</em> mutations or a strong family history of breast/ovarian carcinoma, but in only 2.5% of non&ndash;high-risk women {26630221}. Papillomas are exceedingly rare tumours</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-25T15:29:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63135,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 172,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-25T15:29:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63136,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 172,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-25T15:29:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63137,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 172,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most adenofibromas are &lt;&nbsp;1&nbsp;cm and many are microscopic; uncommon features include multicentricity and bilaterality {18580316}. Papillomas usually appear as an intraluminal papillary brownish proliferation &lt;&nbsp;3&nbsp;cm in diameter {24442176}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-25T15:29:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63138,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 172,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenofibromas have admixed epithelial and mesenchymal components and may be cystic. Papillomas are typically composed of delicate, branching fibrovascular stalks lined by bland, non-ciliated epithelium. They require differentiation from lesions such as metaplastic papillary tumour and reactive hyperplasia {28700429}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-25T15:29:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63139,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 172,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-25T15:29:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
